><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20250701090445+10'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20250701090447+10'00'" name="2d4517b5-308b-4771-bb7d-e95f6dc8c907" page="9" coords="81.013700,285.595000,109.280000,285.595000,81.013700,271.294000,109.280000,271.294000,59.981100,284.702000,76.613100,284.702000,59.981100,271.294000,76.613100,271.294000,113.669000,284.702000,433.164000,284.702000,113.669000,271.294000,433.164000,271.294000,59.981100,271.720000,433.167000,271.720000,59.981100,258.312000,433.167000,258.312000,59.981100,258.738000,433.181000,258.738000,59.981100,245.330000,433.181000,245.330000,59.981100,245.755000,433.181000,245.755000,59.981100,232.347000,433.181000,232.347000,59.981100,232.716000,433.172000,232.716000,59.981100,219.308000,433.172000,219.308000,59.981100,219.734000,433.147000,219.734000,59.981100,206.325000,433.147000,206.325000" rect="59.562100,205.906000,433.600000,286.042000" subject="Comment on Text" title="Alexander Gibson"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-16.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>&#xD;</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>The </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:italic"
>bf</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>PCA extracted distinct patterns that quantified barbell trajectories during the snatch. The statistical analysis showed that barbell trajectory patterns were associated with weightlifting performance and biomechanics. Interpretation of the extracted patterns and statistical results from the correlational analysis suggest that better weightlifting performance was characterised by snatch lifts that exhibited general backward motions and limited forward motion during the first and second pull.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="9" rect="493.228000,193.595000,677.228000,285.595000"
/></highlight
